is very slightly different from mine which is interesting and slightly suspicious so it's possible

00:13:56.020 --> 00:14:02.500
mine doesn't work the same way but anyway let me walk you through it. We're using the SDM32

00:14:02.500 --> 00:14:08.900
blue pill as a debugger interface between the actual computer and the board itself. The board

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:15.940
debugger interface consists of a single line SWS here which is used for both transmit and receive.

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:24.580
The blue pill debugger here is using two pins a six and a seven. A seven is used for receive and

00:14:24.580 --> 00:14:33.140
a six for transmit. There is also b zero here which is used to hard reset the board and of course

00:14:33.140 --> 00:14:42.100
a ground interface. The protocol is weird and extremely bespoke. Each bit is transferred as five

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:50.420
time units. These two red boxes here show a zero bit which is low high high high high

00:14:51.220 --> 00:15:01.460
and a one bit which is low low low low high. There is also occasionally a single low time unit

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:08.740
which is used for handshaking. The conversation is controlled by the debugger interface. The board

00:15:08.820 --> 00:15:16.260
here only talks when the debugger tells it to. This avoids the need for any kind of contention logic.

00:15:17.060 --> 00:15:25.700
The debugger line is pulled high by a pull-up resistor. The two ends can drive it low by connecting

00:15:25.700 --> 00:15:31.540
the line to ground through a transistor. I don't know yet where the pull-up resistor is. It could be

00:15:31.540 --> 00:15:39.140
at either end. That is something I may need to find out. Why isn't it working on my setup? Well

00:15:39.140 --> 00:15:45.540
as always the thing to do is to hook it up to the oscilloscope and see what is actually going on.

