Well this thing is not a ARM processor. It is Tellink's proprietary CPU called the TC32

00:23:50.900 --> 00:23:57.700
and the TC32 is fascinating. This is a file from Tellink's SDK. This is the startup code.

00:23:57.700 --> 00:24:03.060
This is the first thing that executes when the chip comes alive and it contains the usual stuff

00:24:03.060 --> 00:24:08.900
you'd expect to see here like the very first thing in the flash is a jump to the reset routine.

00:24:09.780 --> 00:24:18.980
Here is where the IRQ setup etc etc. Here is the reset routine itself at hex 20 bytes into the

00:24:18.980 --> 00:24:26.980
flash and this is some TC32 machine code. All the TC32 instructions as you can see start with a T.

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:35.780
So T load our clearly loads of value from memory into a register, T-Comp compares to registers,

00:24:36.580 --> 00:24:42.020
jump if greater than or equal to add. This is all looking extremely familiar if you're used to

00:24:42.020 --> 00:24:50.020
these small embedded risk machines. In fact possibly two familiar. Let me show you their compiler.

00:24:50.660 --> 00:24:57.780
Luckily there are Linux binaries available. TC32, LGCC and it is a GCC port,

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:03.940
although there isn't any source available. If I ask it for target specific options,

00:25:04.500 --> 00:25:09.860
we get some stuff. Here is the option to enable TC32 code generation.

00:25:10.820 --> 00:25:19.060
Here is an option for modifying the ARM thumb stack frame. Here are some stuff for the APCS

00:25:19.060 --> 00:25:27.300
ARM calling convention stuff. In fact as far as I can tell I think their back end is basically

00:25:27.380 --> 00:25:34.980
the ARM back end with a few minor adjustments. I suspect that all they've done is done a custom

00:25:34.980 --> 00:25:42.180
assembler and then changed the code generator to produce these instructions rather than the

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:49.220
equivalent thumb instructions. So let's try an experiment. I will pull some code out of this file

00:25:50.420 --> 00:25:54.980
and convert it to thumb and then we'll assemble the two and see what they look like.

00:25:57.300 --> 00:26:05.460
So here it is. This is the TC32 code. This is the thumb code and yes I did just search and

00:26:05.460 --> 00:26:14.900
replace all the instruction names for the thumb equivalent. Here is the disassembly of the TC32 code.

00:26:14.900 --> 00:26:24.340
So here is that first TLoDAR which turns into 0910 etc all the way down. Here is the disassembly

00:26:24.340 --> 00:26:30.420
of the thumb code and you will see that the actual bytes generated are very nearly the same.

00:26:31.540 --> 00:26:38.580
In fact as far as I can tell there's only like one maybe two bits of difference between the

00:26:38.580 --> 00:26:47.380
thumb instructions and the TC32 instructions which means I'm pretty sure that TC32 is basically

00:26:47.380 --> 00:26:55.220
just a subset of thumb with the op codes rearranged just enough that you can't run thumb code on

00:26:55.220 --> 00:27:02.340
the TC32 processor. This isn't necessarily a bad idea. The thumb instruction set is very good.

00:27:02.340 --> 00:27:08.340
It's spectacularly dense producing very small programs. If you're going to base your own instruction

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:14.740
set on something you could do a lot worse than copy thumb but this does seem to be kind of blatant.

00:27:15.700 --> 00:27:18.660
On the plus side it does make the code quite easy to read.

